{# The Kyū Unit #}
<section>
    <h2>The Kyū (Q) Unit</h2>

    <p>At the heart of unit.gl is the <strong>Kyū</strong> (級, Japanese for "grade" or "level"), a micro-unit that
        provides pixel-level precision while remaining scalable and accessible.</p>

    <div class="api-signature">1Q = 0.0625rem = 1/16rem ≈ 1px (at 16px base font size)</div>

    <p>The Q unit bridges the gap between pixel-perfect design and responsive, accessible layouts:</p>

    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Context</th>
                <th>1Q Equals</th>
                <th>Use Case</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Digital (16px base)</td>
                <td>1px</td>
                <td>Screen layouts, web interfaces</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Digital (scaled)</td>
                <td>Scales with rem</td>
                <td>Accessible, user-preference aware</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Print</td>
                <td>0.25mm</td>
                <td>Physical media, typography</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="alert alert--tip">
        <p class="alert__title">Why REM-based?</p>
        <p>Using rem ensures your layouts respect user font size preferences, making your designs accessible by default.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
